    "product_id": "9bf0ddec-cad8-48bb-9679-0b756e008ee7",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 190749\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1249 AM PDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* After mostly dry and low impact weather today, a deepening trough \n  will impact the region midweek with widespread rain and mountain \n  snow chances, gusty winds, and below normal temperatures. \n  \n* Increasing confidence for a dry conditions and warming\n  temperatures this weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through the Weekend.\n\nToday will feature near normal temperatures with abundant sunshine. \nOther than an isolated shower or thunderstorm in the terrain of \nnorthern Mohave County this afternoon, it will be dry through \nTuesday morning. Things change quickly starting Tuesday afternoon as \na deepening upper level trough approaches the region. Gusty winds will \ndevelop Tuesday afternoon in Inyo County and the southern Great Basin \ndue to strengthen southerly flow, which will then spread across the \nregion on Wednesday. Gusts over 40 MPH and wind impacts will be \nisolated with widespread 25 to 35 MPH gusts expected. Precipitation \nwill start as early as Tuesday afternoon as increasing moisture and \nupper level diffluence set up over the Southern Great Basin. \nWidespread precipitation chances are possible at times Wednesday \nthrough much of Thursday as the upper level trough moves into south-\ncentral California. With strong forcing and anomalous moisture in \nplace, moderate to isolated heavy rain is possible and could lead to \nrain impacts, especially in southeast Nevada and western Arizona \nwhere PWATs will push 250-300% of normal. There continues to be \nsignificant uncertainty in exact details as a lot will depend on how \nthe ingredients come together- the difference between even the 25th \nto 75th QPF percentiles remains large. Snow amounts continue to \nfluctuate as well, though increasing confidence that snow levels \nwill remain at or above 9000ft and the main concern will be the high \nterrain of the Sierra. Near normal temperatures on Tuesday, cooler \nthan normal temperatures will accompany the system as it shifts \nthrough the region. \n\nThe trough should exit Thursday or Friday as it continues on to the \neast. Precipitation chances will end west to east as it does so as \ndrier air filters in behind it. By this weekend, PWATs will  be much \nlower and dry northwesterly flow should be in place- increasing \nconfidence for a dry weekend. Temperatures will remain cooler than \nnormal on cool northerly flow, but should warm from the temperatures \nassociated with the peak of the rain and the system overhead.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds \nwill follow typical daily directional patterns, with speeds \ngenerally remaining under 8 knots through Monday. VFR conditions \nwill prevail through Monday, with FEW to SCT mid- and high-level \nclouds and bases AOA 10kft AGL expected. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds follow typical \ndaily directional patterns through Monday. Speeds remain under 8 \nknots in most locations. One exception will be in the northern Owens \nValley, including KBIH, where southerly winds will increase to \naround 10 knots with a few gusts to 20 knots Monday afternoon. VFR \nconditions will prevail area-wide through Monday, with occasional \nmid- and high-level clouds with bases AOA 10kft AGL. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
